A||||4||JohnnyK||Whats possible if I buy a 2.8 Fiero?||||1||
Z||000000||JohnnyK||08-31-2000||07:55 PM||||I have a duke now, and I heard the pistons and crank, etc will not take anything (nitrous, turbo, etc). How does the 2.8 stand? I want to snag maybe a GT and see how much power I can get out of that or a 3.1. Is it possible to push 200hp without any modifications (without adding a turbo, or especially nitrous. I don't like that at all). Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000001||Sillhouette.com||08-31-2000||08:17 PM||||If you don't like turbo maybe a supercharger that will get you the 200 HP. No AC and depending where you live you have to install it.  The install is a piece of cake.  As far as what you can do to a 2.8 is the same answer as what you can do to a Fiero.  With the proper financial massaging anything is possible.  <P>Later||||reg||1
Z||000002||JohnnyK||08-31-2000||08:37 PM||||What i mean, is I heard that the 2.5 can't handle much without changing the pistons and stuff. Is the 2.8 stuff much stronger, or would I have to change them too?||||reg||1
Z||000003||Sillhouette.com||08-31-2000||08:49 PM||||The reliability of your engine is directly related on how radical you want to get.  With only a 6psi supercharger no internal parts need changing.  (Of course if you have an engine that has 200K on it even a clean air filter would be too much strain.)  I also witnessed succesfull usage of a 50HP shot of NOS on a stock engine without any adverse effects on reliability.  The 2.8 is a good reliable engine.  Just change the oil regularly make sure it does not overheat and you'll have countless hours of speeding fun.  <P>Cheers||||reg||1
Z||000004||JohnnyK||08-31-2000||08:57 PM||||So what you are saying is the internals are more reliable than that of the Iron Duck?||||reg||1
